# Copy this file to ".env" in the project directory (same folder as Main.go).
#
# Run:  go run Main.go   from this directory, OR set DOTENV_PATH to this file's full path
#       if you start the program from somewhere else, e.g.
#         export DOTENV_PATH=/Users/you/go-projects/QualificationReport/.env
# Double-quoted values: godotenv treats \ and $ specially (e.g. $USER expands). If your
# password contains $ or backslashes, use single quotes, SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD_FILE, or B64.
# This program loads ".env" with Overload so values here win over empty IDE placeholders.
# If you use Cursor/VS Code launch.json, remove blank SNOWFLAKE_* entries or rely on .env only.
#
# --- Password (pick ONE approach; the app URL-escapes for Snowflake automatically) ---
#
# 1) Plain in .env. Use single quotes if the password contains double quotes — e.g.
#      SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD='...your..."...chars...'
#    (godotenv treats everything between the quotes literally; no URL-encoding needed.)
#    If the password contains a single quote ', use option 2 or 3 instead.
#    Double-quoted values support \" for a literal " inside.
# SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD=...
#
# 2) Password in a separate file (best for messy characters). File should contain
#    ONLY the password, one line, no quotes. Path can be absolute.
# SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD_FILE=/absolute/path/to/snowflake_password.txt
#
# 3) Base64 (no quoting headaches). Produce with:  printf %s 'YOUR_RAW_PASSWORD' | base64
#    (macOS/Linux). Put the single-line output in SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD_B64.
# SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD_B64=

SNOWFLAKE_USER=
SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD=
SNOWFLAKE_ACCOUNT=
SNOWFLAKE_DATABASE=
SNOWFLAKE_SCHEMA=
SNOWFLAKE_WAREHOUSE=

# Optional session role
# SNOWFLAKE_ROLE=

# Optional output path (default: QualificationReport.xlsx)
# REPORT_OUTPUT_PATH=
